win32u: Have the owner recompute its surface region when a window of another process changes.

The owner of a surface clips it around the windows of other processes
inside it, but only recomputed the clip for changes to its own windows.
When the embedded window moved, appeared or resized (a WebView2 shown in a
docked panel), the old clip stayed, and the next flush covered it. Post
WM_WINE_UPDATEWINDOWSTATE to the window instead of ignoring it, so its
owner recomputes the region.
---
 dlls/win32u/window.c | 11 ++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/dlls/win32u/window.c b/dlls/win32u/window.c
index bd73e23..6b07ceb 100644
--- a/dlls/win32u/window.c
+++ b/dlls/win32u/window.c
@@ -2107,7 +2107,16 @@ static void update_surface_region( HWND hwnd )
     HRGN region, shape = 0;
     RECT visible;
 
-    if (!win || win == WND_DESKTOP || win == WND_OTHER_PROCESS) return;
+    /* A window of ours changed inside another process's surface window (an
+     * embedded WebView2 in its host's panel): that process clips its surface
+     * around our window, so it has to recompute the clip, or its next flush
+     * covers what we just drew. */
+    if (win == WND_OTHER_PROCESS)
+    {
+        NtUserPostMessage( hwnd, WM_WINE_UPDATEWINDOWSTATE, 0, 0 );
+        return;
+    }
+    if (!win || win == WND_DESKTOP) return;
     if (!win->surface) goto done;
 
     if (get_window_region( hwnd, FALSE, &shape, &visible )) goto done;
